    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-15654",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/7fbc79b1d259f645a8296be12c2748e5abb05d02217f3628e24ef20781f06c71.png)\n\n \n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the following division problem. \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/a6e6d8c0ba796c8226cc5931d1519010ac1b1606293b3c5a5cf8c8b268a37d15.svg)",
      "worked_solution": "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $\\color{blue}4$ wholes. Each whole is split into *fifths*.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/2096f3451d19fc44bccca9ebe3033e9af29878aa928ad05b102e391be3f76105.png)\n\n \n\n- The fifths are grouped, and each group contains ${\\color{red}{\\dfrac{2}{5}}}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/96bc693ffe7fa65c90758e0d86a7176b19f7a279cfff12607926e0350448fe42.png)\n\n \n\n- There are $\\color{purple} 10$ groups in total.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/1ebdc6bce6be33bbdd5fd7ff79a562c22e68103cebc8f0ded5ddac6833633989.png)\n\n \n\nSo, if we divide $\\color{blue}4$ wholes into groups of ${\\color{red}{\\dfrac{2}{5}}},$ we get ${\\color{purple}{10}}$ groups in total.\n\nTherefore, the model represents the following division problem:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144297/assets/d0d7d29b13dfc358b34ce869e927ebbda919dca0267e8c2289b012d0aa2ab8ac.svg)\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{red}\\dfrac{2}{5}}.$",
